[
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-of-one-datom-costs-its-nodes"
  :seon.task/plan "Where it stands on main's pins (Datahike 8250357f, which carries the node addresses of one digest, a leaf's address taken from the leaf as stored, the parent's commit-graph entry not read back, and the out-of-line pass that reads the store's collection generation once a flush and walks only collection values; docs/forks.md#datahike). Measured 2026-11-10/11 by Deep fixes' helpers (notes honest-write-phases and nodes-per-write, 2026-11-11). Direct ``d/transact`` of one datom on a 28.7k-datom LMDB store (history, content-addressed nodes, the :sync writer, median of 31): 171k ticks, 787 KB, 6.4 nodes, 9.4 keys, 0.93 ms; designed about 150k. Through root's writer (main of 250-286k datoms, 40 writes): 23-26 nodes and 26-29 keys a write (27 on the standing root, whose history trees are a level deeper), 53 KB of zstd values, ``datahike.writer/transact-batch!`` 597-614k ticks and 3.2 MB, ``seon.root/transact-main!``'s deref p50 5.2-6.0 ms; of the write: the apply about 80k; the flush about 190-210k (out-of-line 89-110k, address hashing about 100k); the commit id 28k; konserve's multi-assoc about 330k (serializing each node and meta about 120k, store keys about 54k, the 27 meta maps (each key's own :key, :last-write and :revision, 144 B: not a constant, as this entry first said; their cost is not measured apart), zstd 26k, the LMDB puts 27k); the LMDB commit about 0.35 ms of waiting; about 0.4 ms of thread hops; the sync (F_FULLFSYNC, 3.7-7 ms) is the flusher's, off the answer's path. Why 25 nodes for one datom: 13 are the transaction entity's (``:db/txInstant`` 6, `:seon.tx/runtime` 1, `:seon.tx/branch` 5 with its avet path, sharing an aevt branch), 4-6 the history trees', 8 the floor (one root-to-leaf path in eavt and aevt). A commit costs about 112k ticks fixed and 18.2k a node. Since measured, `:seon.tx/branch` is no longer indexed by value and `:seon.root/collected` keeps no history (docs/storage.md#what-a-datom-costs-to-add), which the same measure puts at 5 and 4 nodes fewer on that write: not measured again on main. Group commit shares the transaction entity's nodes: ten writes in one commit wrote 31 nodes, not 250, 8x fewer commit ticks. A larger branching factor costs more (128: 5 fewer nodes, +21% bytes, +7% ticks at 250k datoms; 64 is the cheapest of 64-512 at every size measured). Left, largest first: the store write (tasks konserve.impl.defaults/a-value-already-serialized-is-stored-as-it-is and konserve.impl.defaults/a-store-key-costs-one-digest, whose first two changes are on konserve 1119607: 353.8k to 335.3k ticks on a direct one-datom commit; what it leaves is the digest shim in jolt.crypto, 938 ticks a digest against 353); the address hashing (one native digest of a node's bytes, inferred 35-40k); the commit id (28k: hasch of the stored commit; the audit recomputes it from content, so it does not change without the audit); the commit record's ``:cache-revisions`` (90% of its 4.3 KB, 14k ticks a commit). The owner's decisions, not small fixes: dropping a tx-meta datom (facts Seon records), and whether content ids that scatter new entities across every index are worth the pages they dirty. Proof when done: a cost proof of a one-datom commit by nodes written, on a store on disk, sizes by store depth."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-fork-unit-in-one-process-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12. All 108 fork units run in one process stop at ``datahike.test.tx-instant-monotonic-test``: Jolt's detector ends the run with 'deadlock: every fiber and thread of this process waits, and nothing can wake one', 1,018 live fibers of 2,582 held, most parked on alt-handler under ``datahike.writer/create-thread``. The unit passes alone, and ``bin/forks`` runs each unit in its own process, so no verdict is red; the finding is that connections of earlier units leave writer fibers alive and a later unit then waits for ever. Reproduction (read-only, about ten minutes, one heavy run): ``. bin/env; target/jolt/jolt -A:dhtest run test/forks/run.jolt $(grep -v '^;' test/forks/baseline.edn | awk 'NF{print $1}')``. Evidence, kept outside the repository in the sessions' notes folder: ``deepfixes-evidence/forksall-hang-evidence.log`` (the detector's dump) and ``forksall-hang-sample.txt``. Not known: which unit leaves the fibers (count live fibers after each unit; the first that leaves one names it), and whether the wait is on a writer released by another unit (a transact on a released connection now raises, Datahike 66b5e71c) or on a writer never released. Seon's own path may hold the same fault where one process opens and releases many connections (a test process, the standing system over days): check that a released connection's writer fibers end. Proof when done: a fork unit that opens and releases n connections and asserts the live fiber count returns to its start."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
]
